# Kira stack conventions

Kira is a Kotlin/JVM compiler (Gradle, ANTLR) that lowers a small OOP language
to ISO C17 source (C-as-IR). Native toolchain finishes the job.

## Build and test

- Gradle wrapper: `gradle-9.5.1`, Kotlin 2.3.20, ANTLR 4.13.2.
- Java toolchain: `jvmToolchain(17)`. CI pins Temurin 17; a newer local JDK is
  fine because Gradle resolves the 17 toolchain.
- Verify gate for any compiler change: `./gradlew test`.
- CLI install: `./gradlew installDist` -> `build/install/kira/bin/kira` and
  `.../kira-lsp`. Rebuild after codegen changes before using the CLI.
- `./gradlew run` compiles the `test_kira/` smoke project (workingDir is set
  to `test_kira` in build.gradle.kts).

## Parser backends

- Default parser is LEGACY (`KiraSourceParsers`). Select ANTLR with
  `-Dkira.parser=antlr` or `KIRA_PARSER=antlr`.
- ANTLR backend is syntax validation only; the LEGACY backend builds the AST
  that semantic analysis and codegen consume. Do not assume the two agree.

## Commit conventions

- Author every commit as `Ming <daoxe@users.noreply.github.com>` (the
  run_shell wrapper injects `--author` automatically).
- Commit coherent verified units with a short subject line
  (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `chore:` prefixes).
- `.ming/` is gitignored in this repo: rules and skills here are local to
  Ming and never committed.
- Committed generated artifacts (example C snapshots, expected.txt) are
  intentional; see snapshots rule.
